issues.body:
I think this is an issue as it does not account for mobs that have different moving animations on land, i.e amphibious animals like penguins waddling on land. This isn't clear at first without reading the code. Although it is trivial for a dev to add a check for if the entity is in water or not.

Below is what geckolib has
```
public static <T extends GeoAnimatable> AnimationController<T> genericSwimController(T entity) {
		return new AnimationController<T>(entity, "Swim", 0, state -> {
			if (state.isMoving())
				return state.setAndContinue(SWIM);

			return PlayState.STOP;
		});
	}
```
```
	public static <T extends GeoAnimatable> AnimationController<T> genericWalkController(T animatable) {
		return new AnimationController<T>(animatable, "Walk", 0, state -> {
			if (state.isMoving())
				return state.setAndContinue(WALK);

			return PlayState.STOP;
		});
	}
```

I'm trying to play the walking animation only while the entity is moving and stop it when the entity stands still.

isMoving() works correctly and returns false when the entity stops. However, the animation keeps playing and does not stop, even though the controller reaches PlayState.STOP.

@Override
    public void registerControllers(AnimatableManager.ControllerRegistrar controllers) {
        controllers.add(new AnimationController<>(test -> {
            if (test.isMoving()){
                return test.setAndContinue(DefaultAnimations.WALK);
            }
            return PlayState.STOP; //ignores even when reached
        }));
    }


I've verified that the code enters the return PlayState.STOP branch, but the animation continues playing as if the return value is ignored.

