Summary:

The transformToBone() method in RenderUtil.java applies bone transforms in leaf-to-root order instead of root-to-leaf order, causing incorrect positioning when using addPerBoneRender() callbacks with animated bones.

Location:
software.bernie.geckolib.util.RenderUtil.transformToBone() (common module)

The Bug:

```
public static void transformToBone(PoseStack poseStack, GeoBone bone) {
    final List<GeoBone> boneQueue = new ObjectArrayList<>();
    GeoBone parent = bone;

    boneQueue.add(bone);

    while ((parent = parent.parent()) != null) {
        boneQueue.add(parent);
    }
    // Queue is now: [bone, parent, grandparent, root]

    for (GeoBone bone2 : boneQueue) {  // Iterates: bone → parent → root (WRONG)
        prepMatrixForBone(poseStack, bone2);
    }
}
```


The queue is built as [bone, parent, grandparent, root] and iterated forward, so transforms are applied leaf -> root. However, matrix multiplication requires root -> leaf order for correct hierarchical positioning.

How to Reproduce:

Create a GeoRenderLayer that uses addPerBoneRender() to render a bone with custom texture
Animate the bone and its parent bones
Observe that animations appear incorrect/offset

The Fix:
Iterate the queue in reverse order:

```
for (int i = boneQueue.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    prepMatrixForBone(poseStack, boneQueue.get(i));
}
```

